[Comments]
Count=21
1=This profile shows no overt signs of instablity in the snowpack here. A weakness low in the pack required a 
2=large trigger to release, hence there is decent bridging through the 1F - P midpack. Much of the faceted layers 
3=are beginning to show signs of rounding (particularly in top half of snowpack) with the recent warmer temps. 
4=
5=Snow depths in the forecast area range from 45 cm at lower elevations to 150 - 200 in loaded alpine areas. 
6=Suncrusts are developing on south and west aspects. There is some limited wind effect in alpine areas, with 
7=some recent wind transport. Faceting is still evident low in the snowpack below the remnants of the October 
8=crust, and this is still reacting to stab. tests, although in the M - H range. A small amount of whumphing has 
9=been observed in shallower snowpack areas at treeeline. Below treeline snow is still unconsolidated facets, 
10=although the midpack is trying to gain strength with the warmer temps (still 40 cm ski pen. in these areas).
11=
12=The Parker's area has received 31 cm of new snow over the past two weeks. Temperatures have been above 
13=seasonal values, with highs of +1.5 at Parker's to +7 at lower elevations (Sunwapta), generally o/n lows have 
14=been -9 to -14. Winds have been much lighter than most of the winter, except for Feb 15 (45 km/hr for 9 hrs).
15=
16=Avalanche activity was quite significant with the new snow and onset of warm weather at the start of this two 
17=week period, particularly on south and west facing slopes. This has been slowly diminishing, and very few 
18=natural avalanches have been observed over the last two days.
19=
20=Snowpack stability deteriorated after the 15 cm HN on Feb 17 (along with increase in temp.), but it has been 
21=slowly improving with warm sunny days and cool nights.
Stability=G
